  "worked_solution": "The range of ${2}^{-x}$ is \n\n$$\n{2}^{-x}>0.\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches do not affect the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{2}^{2-x}>0.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $-3$ and then subtracting $1,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{2-x} & >0 \\\\ -3(2)^{2-x} & <0 \\\\ -3(2)^{2-x}-1 & <0-1 \\\\ y & <-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range is $y\\in (-\\infty ,-1).$ \n\nWe can also see that this is true from the graph of the function. The graph of $f(x)=-3(2)^{2-x}-1$ is shown below.  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943819/assets/88b75fabc306eb09a7ca63c909de3272cc776bf808e29da4cc08ae2c5a53e192.png)",
